# =============================================================================
# God's Eye View — environment variables
# =============================================================================
#
# PRODUCTION POSTURE (container / internet-reachable deployment)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS FILE IS THE ONLY WAY TO SET KEYS IN PRODUCTION.
#
# The in-app key editor (the "POWER UP" chip -> Provider Settings, backed by
# /api/setup/status and /api/setup/keys) is DISABLED IN PRODUCTION BY DESIGN.
# The `gev-key-setup` plugin gates itself with
#   apply: command === 'serve' && !isPreview        (vite.config.js)
# and registers only `configureServer`, never `configurePreviewServer`, so Vite
# drops it from the resolved plugin list under `vite preview`. The production
# entrypoint (server/production-server.mjs) bridges dev-only proxies onto the
# preview server but deliberately does NOT resurrect that plugin. Net effect:
# in production nothing can write credentials over HTTP, and the panel is inert.
#
# So: copy this file to `.env`, fill it in, and restart the container. Do not
# expect to paste keys into the UI on the deployed host — that path is closed.
# (In LOCAL DEV, `npm run dev`, the panel DOES work and writes this checkout's
# .env for you with owner-only permissions. Keys you supply yourself via shell
# env or macOS Keychain are shown as "configured externally" and never touched.)
#
# NEVER COMMIT A FILLED-IN .env. Keep real values out of this .example file.
#
# KEY EXPOSURE MODEL
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLIENT-EXPOSED (2 variables, and only these 2): GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY and
# CESIUM_ION_TOKEN are injected into the browser bundle on purpose, via the
# `define:` block at the bottom of vite.config.js. They WILL be readable in
# devtools. Restrict/scope them at the provider instead of trying to hide them
# (see SECURITY.md).
#
# Vite also auto-exposes ANY variable whose name starts with `VITE_`. The three
# VITE_* variables below are display/plumbing settings only — never put a
# credential behind a VITE_ prefix.
#
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY: every other key here is read only inside vite.config.js
# middleware, used to build an outbound upstream request, and never written
# into a response body. The browser talks to same-origin /api/* proxies.
#
# SPEND PROTECTION (read this before exposing the host publicly)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is NO authentication in front of /api/*. Anyone who can reach this
# server can make it spend YOUR provider quota:
#   /api/realtime/token     -> mints an OpenAI Realtime session (most expensive)
#   /api/openai/hud-summary -> an OpenAI Responses call every 15s per open tab
#   /api/google/nearby-places, /api/google/text-search -> Google Places billing
#   /api/tomtom/flow/...    -> TomTom tile requests
# The GEV_RATELIMIT_* throttles below DEFAULT TO UNLIMITED. On a publicly
# reachable host that is dangerous — set them (recommended values are provided
# UNCOMMENTED below), AND put real access control in front of the site (an
# Nginx Proxy Manager Access List with HTTP Basic auth), AND set provider-side
# billing caps. The in-app throttles are a backstop, not a billing cap.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Maps / Places
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: direct Google Photorealistic 3D Tiles and GEV place search.
# Without it, a Cesium ion token can still load ion-hosted Google 3D; with
# neither credential, the app starts on keyless Esri World Imagery with OSM
# available in the map tray and as the automatic provider-failure fallback.
# The SAME key also powers the server-side /api/google/nearby-places and
# /api/google/text-search proxies (metered Places API — real money).
# Default: unset (keyless mode).
# CLIENT-EXPOSED — but DO NOT reach for the usual HTTP-referrer restriction here
# without reading this first, because on this key it is self-defeating.
#
# One key, two very different callers:
#   - the BROWSER calls maps.googleapis.com directly (geocoding), and DOES send
#     a Referer header, so a referrer restriction works on those calls;
#   - the SERVER calls places.googleapis.com and the Street View static endpoint
#     from inside vite.config.js, and server-side fetch sends NO Referer at all,
#     so a referrer restriction REJECTS exactly the metered calls you most want
#     to keep working. The failure looks like a confusing 403 from /api/google/*.
#
# Recommended for this single-key deployment:
#   1. Application restriction: NONE. (A referrer rule breaks the server proxies;
#      an IP rule cannot be written, since the browser half has no fixed IP.)
#   2. API restriction: YES — limit the key to only the APIs this app uses
#      (Map Tiles / Photorealistic 3D Tiles, Geocoding, Places API (New),
#      Street View Static). This is what actually bounds the blast radius.
#   3. Per-API quotas: APIs & Services -> Quotas, cap each one.
#   4. Budget alert: Billing -> Budgets & alerts.
#   5. Keep strangers off the site entirely with the reverse-proxy access list —
#      that, not the key restriction, is the real control here.
# If you would rather have the referrer lock, it takes TWO Google keys (one
# browser-restricted, one server-restricted) and a code change — the app reads
# only this one variable today.
GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# Per-IP rate limit for the Google Places cost endpoints
# (/api/google/nearby-places and /api/google/text-search), in requests/minute.
# Default: UNLIMITED — unset, 0, or non-numeric means no throttling at all.
# SERVER-SIDE.
# Set for this deployment. 60/min covers normal annotation + voice place lookups
# for one user with room to spare, and caps a scripted abuser at 60 Places calls
# a minute instead of "as fast as they can loop".
# NOTE ON SEMANTICS: the limiter keys on req.socket.remoteAddress and
# deliberately IGNORES X-Forwarded-For (vite.config.js clientKey()). Behind
# Cloudflare -> Nginx Proxy Manager -> container, every request arrives from the
# NPM container's IP, so this is effectively ONE GLOBAL BUCKET, not per-visitor.
# That is safer (an attacker cannot mint fresh quota by rotating IPs) but it
# means your own browsing shares the same budget — size it accordingly.
# In-memory and process-local: it resets on restart. Over-limit requests get a
# sanitized 429. It is NOT a billing cap — also set Google Cloud Console ->
# Billing -> Budgets & alerts, plus per-API quotas under APIs & Services -> Quotas.
GEV_RATELIMIT_GOOGLE_PER_MIN=60


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cesium ion
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: Google Photorealistic 3D Tiles through Cesium ion, Bing world
# imagery, and Cesium World Terrain. The free Community plan is for eligible
# personal/non-commercial use and has quotas; check current Cesium terms. A
# direct Google key above is still required for GEV place search.
# Default: unset (ion layers unavailable; Esri/OSM fallback still works).
# CLIENT-EXPOSED — use a public assets:read token with URL restrictions.
CESIUM_ION_TOKEN=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenAI — Realtime voice + HUD summary
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: OpenAI Realtime voice control and the HUD one-line summary.
# Do not prefix with VITE_. Default: unset (voice + HUD summary disabled).
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY. The raw key never leaves the server: /api/realtime/token
# sends it as an Authorization header to
# https://api.openai.com/v1/realtime/client_secrets and returns only the
# short-lived EPHEMERAL client secret (an `ek_...` value) to the browser, which
# then negotiates WebRTC directly with OpenAI.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Realtime model for the standard voice tier.
# Default: gpt-realtime-2 (VOICE_MODELS.standard.id in src/voice/voiceCost.js).
# SERVER-SIDE.
OPENAI_REALTIME_MODEL=gpt-realtime-2

# Cheaper voice tier, used when the GEV MIC panel's MINI toggle is on.
# Model ids and prices are external facts that drift — if OpenAI moves this id,
# override it here rather than editing src/voice/voiceCost.js.
# Default: gpt-realtime-2.1-mini. SERVER-SIDE.
OPENAI_REALTIME_MODEL_MINI=gpt-realtime-2.1-mini

# Realtime assistant voice. Default: marin. SERVER-SIDE.
OPENAI_REALTIME_VOICE=marin

# Realtime reasoning effort. Default: low. SERVER-SIDE.
OPENAI_REALTIME_REASONING_EFFORT=low

# Keep only a short conversational window. Current map state is fetched live.
# Clamped server-side to 1000..12000. Default: 3000. SERVER-SIDE.
OPENAI_REALTIME_CONTEXT_TOKENS=3000

# Fraction of the context window retained on truncation. Clamped to 0.1..1.
# Default: 0.5. SERVER-SIDE.
OPENAI_REALTIME_CONTEXT_RETENTION=0.5

# Model behind /api/openai/hud-summary (the five-word HUD line).
# Default: gpt-5-nano. SERVER-SIDE.
OPENAI_HUD_SUMMARY_MODEL=gpt-5-nano

# Per-IP rate limit for the OpenAI cost endpoints (/api/realtime/token and
# /api/openai/hud-summary), in requests/minute.
# Default: UNLIMITED — unset, 0, or non-numeric means no throttling at all.
# SERVER-SIDE.
# Set for this deployment. Budgeting: the HUD polls hud-summary every 15s per
# open tab (4/min/tab) and a voice session mints one token per start, so 30/min
# leaves plenty of headroom for one person on a couple of devices while capping
# a stranger who found the URL. THIS IS THE ENDPOINT THAT COSTS THE MOST —
# leaving it unlimited on a public host means an unauthenticated visitor can
# open Realtime voice sessions against your account in a loop.
# Same single-global-bucket caveat as the Google limiter above (the limiter
# ignores X-Forwarded-For, so all proxied traffic shares one counter).
# In-memory, resets on restart, sanitized 429 over the cap. NOT a billing cap —
# also set OpenAI platform -> Settings -> Limits (usage limits).
GEV_RATELIMIT_OPENAI_PER_MIN=30


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenSky (live aircraft)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: OpenSky auth mode — oauth | basic | auto | anon.
# Default: oauth (requires the client credentials below). An invalid value logs
# a warning and falls back to oauth. Set "anon" to skip aircraft auth
# (rate-limited, but works with no credentials). SERVER-SIDE.
OPENSKY_AUTH_MODE=oauth

# Optional: OpenSky OAuth2 client credentials (opensky-network.org account
# dashboard -> API client). Used by OPENSKY_AUTH_MODE=oauth|auto.
# Default: unset. SERVER-SIDE ONLY — exchanged for a bearer token server-side;
# never sent to the browser.
OPENSKY_CLIENT_ID=
OPENSKY_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Optional: legacy OpenSky HTTP Basic credentials, used only when
# OPENSKY_AUTH_MODE=basic (or auto, as a fallback). Default: unset.
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY.
# OPENSKY_USERNAME=
# OPENSKY_PASSWORD=

# Optional: OpenSky credentials JSON file path. Read by the shell launchers
# (scripts/dev-fresh.sh, scripts/dev-secure.sh, scripts/opensky-import-client.sh),
# which export CLIENT_ID/CLIENT_SECRET from it. Not read by the server itself,
# and not used by the Docker entrypoint. Default: unset. SERVER-SIDE.
# OPENSKY_CREDENTIALS_FILE=/path/to/credentials.json


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Launch Library 2 (rocket launches)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: higher Launch Library 2 request allowance. Public access works
# without a token. Default: unset (anonymous tier).
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY — sent as an upstream Authorization header by /api/launches.
LL2_API_TOKEN=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server binding
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TCP port the server listens on. Default: 4173.
# The container publishes/forwards this port; keep it in sync with the Nginx
# Proxy Manager "Forward Port". SERVER-SIDE.
PORT=4173

# Network binding.
#
# IN DOCKER YOU MUST USE 0.0.0.0. docker-compose sets HOST=0.0.0.0 explicitly.
# The historical default is `localhost`, which binds only the loopback inside
# the container's own network namespace — the port then answers nothing from
# outside, and Nginx Proxy Manager gets a connection-refused / 502 with no
# obvious cause. If the site is unreachable through eye.dinoclyde.com but the
# container is "running", check this first.
#
# Default when unset: localhost (vite.config.js `server.host`), except
# server/production-server.mjs which defaults to 0.0.0.0 for exactly this reason.
# SERVER-SIDE.
#
# Binding 0.0.0.0 means anything that can route to the container can reach every
# key-brokering /api/* proxy. There is no auth in the app. That is acceptable
# ONLY because the container is not published to the LAN directly and Nginx
# Proxy Manager sits in front with an Access List — see the spend-protection
# note at the top of this file.
HOST=0.0.0.0
# HOST=localhost   # local dev on your own machine only


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NASA FIRMS (active fires)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: NASA FIRMS live active fires. Free key:
# https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/api/map_key/
# Default: unset — the FIRMS Active Fires layer is empty ("KEY REQUIRED") and
# /api/firms returns 503 {error:'no_key'} without touching upstream.
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY — never reaches the browser; the client polls /api/firms.
# Free but transaction-limited upstream; the proxy caches and reports remaining
# transactions via /api/firms/status.
# FIRMS_MAP_KEY=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Live AIS vessels (AISStream)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browser fetch path. Keep same-origin so the private stream key stays
# server-side. Default: /api/ais-live.
# CLIENT-EXPOSED (VITE_ prefix) — a path, not a credential.
VITE_AIS_LIVE_API_URL=/api/ais-live

# Client render cap. The server can keep a larger AISStream cache, but drawing
# every global vessel at once can overwhelm WebGL on laptops. Clamped client-side
# to 500..50000. Default: 12000.
# CLIENT-EXPOSED (VITE_ prefix) — a display setting, not a credential.
VITE_AIS_LIVE_MAX_ROWS=12000

# Active label cap after screen-space clustering. Labels exist for all rendered
# ships, but only clustered winners are shown to keep text readable.
# Default: 900 (clamped to 0..VITE_AIS_LIVE_MAX_ROWS).
# CLIENT-EXPOSED (VITE_ prefix) — a display setting, not a credential.
VITE_AIS_LIVE_LABEL_MAX_ROWS=900

# Private AISStream key for the server-side websocket. Do not prefix with VITE_.
# Default: unset — /api/ais-live answers 503 and the vessel layer stays empty.
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY — sent as the `APIKey` field on the outbound
# wss://stream.aisstream.io subscription; the browser never sees it.
AISSTREAM_API_KEY=

# Optional: override the AISStream websocket endpoint (testing / mirrors).
# Default: wss://stream.aisstream.io/v0/stream. SERVER-SIDE.
# AISSTREAM_URL=wss://stream.aisstream.io/v0/stream

# Optional AISStream subscription filters. JSON / comma lists.
# Defaults: worldwide bbox [[[-90,-180],[90,180]]], and the five position/static
# message types shown below. SERVER-SIDE.
# AISSTREAM_BOUNDING_BOXES=[[[-90,-180],[90,180]]]
# AISSTREAM_MESSAGE_TYPES=PositionReport,StandardClassBPositionReport,ExtendedClassBPositionReport,ShipStaticData,StaticDataReport

# Feed watchdog: silence (ms) after which the feed is REPORTED stale; the socket
# is recycled at 2.5x that. Default 120000. The watchdog self-arms only for the
# default worldwide subscription — a narrow filter above can be legitimately
# quiet, so set this explicitly (sized for your filter) to opt back in.
# 0 disables the silence watch entirely. SERVER-SIDE.
# AISSTREAM_SILENCE_TIMEOUT_MS=120000


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TomTom live traffic
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: TomTom live traffic flow (BYOK freemium — free tier is ~50,000 tile
# requests/day, get a key at https://developer.tomtom.com).
# Default: unset. SERVER-SIDE ONLY: the browser fetches same-origin
# /api/tomtom/flow/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf and never sees the key; the proxy never echoes
# the key or the upstream URL, even in errors.
# Keyless fallback: the traffic layer runs its built-in simulation (white dots,
# hardcoded per-road-class speeds) — no key required for the layer to work.
# TOMTOM_API_KEY=

# Soft cap on upstream tile fetches per UTC day — a configurable application
# safety ceiling, not a guarantee of staying within TomTom's free allowance
# (currently 200K tile requests/month: https://docs.tomtom.com/pricing/).
# Counted in .gev-cache/tomtom/budget.json and reset on UTC day change; cache
# hits never count. Over the cap the proxy serves cached/stale tiles, else 429.
# Default: 40000/day. SERVER-SIDE.
# Set for this deployment. 40000 is sized for a shared/trusted install; one
# person browsing traffic will not approach 5000 tiles a day, so this doubles as
# an abuse ceiling for a publicly reachable host. Note this endpoint is NOT
# covered by the GEV_RATELIMIT_* throttles — this budget is its only brake.
TOMTOM_DAILY_TILE_BUDGET=5000


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CCTV layer tuning (advanced)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# All SERVER-SIDE. Defaults are sensible — leave unset unless you're customizing
# the camera source pack. The /api/cctv/media/{id} proxy only fetches URLs that
# came from THIS registry (no client-supplied URL is ever fetched).
# CCTV_SOURCES_FILE=config/cctv_sources.austin.json   # source-pack JSON path. Default: unset
# CCTV_SOURCES_JSON=                                   # inline source-pack JSON (overrides file). Default: unset
# CCTV_AUSTIN_ROWS_URL=                                # override the Austin Open Data catalog URL.
#                                                      # Default: https://data.austintexas.gov/api/views/b4k4-adkb/rows.json?accessType=DOWNLOAD
# CCTV_AUSTIN_MAX_SOURCES=250                          # cap cameras from the Austin catalog. Default 250 (clamped 8..300)
# CCTV_CALTRANS_DISTRICTS=4,7,11,3                     # Caltrans districts to load. Default "4,7,11,3"; empty string disables Caltrans
# CCTV_CALTRANS_MAX_SOURCES=300                        # cap Caltrans cameras. Default 300 (clamped 8..600)
# CCTV_TFL_ENABLED=1                                   # London TfL JamCams. Default 1 (on); "0" disables
# CCTV_TFL_MAX_SOURCES=250                             # cap TfL cameras. Default 250 (clamped 8..600)
# CCTV_MAX_SOURCES=900                                 # overall cap on registered cameras. Default 900 (clamped 8..1200)
# CCTV_PREFER_AUSTIN=1                                 # prioritize Austin cameras in the registry. Default 1 (on); "0" disables
# CCTV_FORCE_AUSTIN=                                   # set to "1" to force Austin-only sources. Default unset (off)
#
# NOT READ BY CURRENT CODE — these two appear in older docs but no longer map to
# any runtime flag. Setting them does nothing. Kept here only so you don't go
# looking for why they had no effect:
# CCTV_AUTO_CALIBRATE=1
# CCTV_DRAPE_MESH=1

# Optional: Transport for London app key. Purely raises TfL's list-endpoint rate
# limit — JamCam frames come from TfL's public S3 bucket, and the 15-minute
# source cache keeps list hits well below anonymous limits anyway.
# Default: unset (anonymous). SERVER-SIDE ONLY — appended as an upstream
# `app_key` query param, never returned to the browser.
# TFL_APP_KEY=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Launcher / provenance markers (set by tooling — do not set these by hand)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GEV_LAUNCHER: which launcher started the process ("pinokio", "dev-fresh").
# Provider Settings uses it to decide which credential store it owns, so it must
# reflect the real launcher. scripts/pinokio-start.mjs and scripts/dev-fresh.sh
# set it; a value in .env would be misleading and is deliberately ignored for
# the boot snapshot. Default: unset. SERVER-SIDE.
# GEV_LAUNCHER=
#
# GEV_KEY_SETUP_EXTERNAL_KEYS: names-only provenance marker (comma-separated env
# var names) emitted by scripts/dev-fresh.sh so Provider Settings can mark
# shell-inherited keys read-only. Contains NO credential material.
# Default: unset. SERVER-SIDE.
# GEV_KEY_SETUP_EXTERNAL_KEYS=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test / QA harness only (scripts/ — never needed in production)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GEV_TEST_VERBOSE=            # verbose unit-test output. Default: unset
# GEV_REQUIRE_ALLOCATION_GATE= # enforce the allocation-gate check in the suite. Default: unset
# QA_BASE_URL=                 # base URL the QA screenshot runner targets. Default: local server
# QA_LABEL=                    # label stamped on QA artifacts. Default: unset
# QA_SHOTS_DIR=                # output directory for QA screenshots. Default: repo-local
# QA_MAP_SOURCE_TRAY_KEYLESS=  # force the keyless map-tray QA scenario. Default: unset
# PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH=   # browser binary for the QA runner. Default: bundled Chromium
